1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:25,199
This focusing charge is for the convenience of the projectionist to set the focus and sound

2
00:00:25,199 --> 00:00:31,919
levels for this film. Following this image the screen will go black and the

3
00:00:31,919 --> 00:00:35,659
film will begin in five seconds.

4
00:00:55,200 --> 00:01:09,060
During the next 30 years, man will consume more than three times the energy consumed

5
00:01:09,060 --> 00:01:20,719
since time began.

6
00:01:20,719 --> 00:01:37,060
every ten years, this country's electrical power needs double.

7
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:43,200
The burden of producing this energy is now carried almost entirely by the burning of

8
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:49,359
of naturally occurring fossil fuels such as coal, oil, and gas.

9
00:01:58,799 --> 00:02:03,079
As these fuels are used up, America's power industry

10
00:02:03,079 --> 00:02:07,900
must be carried more and more by nuclear energy, which

11
00:02:07,900 --> 00:02:13,960
could generate half of all the electricity used in this country by the year 2000.

12
00:02:19,219 --> 00:02:22,400
These energy demands place increased emphasis

13
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:27,240
upon the design and testing of advanced nuclear reactors

14
00:02:27,240 --> 00:02:31,420
to improve the economics of large central station power plants.

15
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:38,099
The majority of all civilian power reactors in use today

16
00:02:38,099 --> 00:02:46,959
burn uranium-235 in a slow neutron reactor.

17
00:02:46,959 --> 00:02:52,560
To depend solely upon fissionable uranium as a fuel source would place significant limits

18
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:57,079
upon our future nuclear fuel reserves.

19
00:02:57,079 --> 00:03:03,759
To overcome this limitation, the Atomic Energy Commission has placed high priority on the

20
00:03:03,759 --> 00:03:16,120
development of fast breeder reactors that produce or breed more fuel than they consume.

21
00:03:16,120 --> 00:03:22,639
This breeding capability in fast reactors would give us virtually an unlimited source

22
00:03:22,639 --> 00:03:32,099
of nuclear fuel.

23
00:03:32,099 --> 00:03:40,459
But between the idea and the reality lies a vast sea of technical and research problems.

24
00:03:40,459 --> 00:03:48,659
And for the last 20 years, nuclear scientists have huddled in the Idaho desert in search

25
00:03:48,659 --> 00:04:00,439
of a critical moment.

26
00:04:00,439 --> 00:04:08,819
This is the Upper Snake River Plain in Idaho, formed over 10,000 years ago by the convulsion

27
00:04:08,819 --> 00:04:18,439
of large molten flows of lava.

28
00:04:18,439 --> 00:04:24,180
The first white men to range over this country were fur trappers, fast on the search for

29
00:04:24,180 --> 00:04:28,019
beaver and elk.

30
00:04:28,019 --> 00:04:36,799
In the early 1800s, large cattle herds were pushed across this area to eastern markets.

31
00:04:36,799 --> 00:04:46,579
The high buttes were early landmarks to men seeking gold.

32
00:04:46,579 --> 00:04:53,620
Under this broad expanse of 900 square miles of open desert, large tracks of lava rock

33
00:04:53,620 --> 00:05:02,259
randomly outcropped here and there to remind us of their volcanic origin.

34
00:05:02,259 --> 00:05:08,860
During World War II, this desolate plain was used for gunnery practice by the Navy.

35
00:05:08,860 --> 00:05:17,500
And in 1949, the atom came into this desert and built the largest, most advanced nuclear

36
00:05:17,500 --> 00:05:23,839
reactor testing station in the world.

37
00:05:23,839 --> 00:05:30,180
The knowledge of tomorrow's reactors comes from research carried out today.

38
00:05:30,180 --> 00:05:41,220
In the 20 years since its inception, 45 experimental reactors have been built in this testing complex.

39
00:05:41,220 --> 00:05:46,600
Many of the totems of proven technology on commercial reactors have come from research

40
00:05:46,600 --> 00:05:53,540
performed at this station.

41
00:05:53,540 --> 00:06:00,120
One of the first reactors built here was experimental breeder reactor number one, which demonstrated

42
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:06,740
the feasibility of fast breeder systems in December 1951.

43
00:06:06,740 --> 00:06:15,300
Now a National Historical Monument, EBR1 was the first reactor to generate electricity.

44
00:06:15,300 --> 00:06:21,540
It contributed important data on the physics and kinetics of reactors and their fuels and

45
00:06:21,540 --> 00:06:27,259
demonstrated the feasibility of using liquid metal as a coolant.

46
00:06:27,259 --> 00:06:33,879
The lessons from EBR 1 were anchor points which led to the construction of experimental

47
00:06:33,879 --> 00:06:39,460
breeder reactor number two in 1961.

48
00:06:39,460 --> 00:06:45,740
This is the principal facility for irradiating fuel samples and structural materials in the

49
00:06:45,740 --> 00:06:50,760
liquid metal fast breeder reactor program.

50
00:06:50,760 --> 00:06:58,080
To achieve the technology and control so necessary for the experimental method, a series of research

51
00:06:58,080 --> 00:07:04,380
machines have been developed to study advanced core designs.

52
00:07:04,380 --> 00:07:08,580
Let's take a look at a fast reactor core.

53
00:07:08,579 --> 00:07:15,000
To approximate the composition, configuration, and performance of this hypothetical core,

54
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:22,639
core halves are gradually built up on a rugged platform in a horizontal plane.

55
00:07:22,639 --> 00:07:35,979
The matrices are loaded on two separate tables, one stationary and the other movable.

56
00:07:35,980 --> 00:07:43,700
After each progressive fuel loading, the tables are remotely drawn together to test the critical

57
00:07:43,700 --> 00:07:46,600
assembly.

58
00:07:46,600 --> 00:07:53,600
The final criticality, or self-sustaining chain reaction, is reached by the slow insertion

59
00:07:53,600 --> 00:08:00,819
of fuel-bearing control rods driven into each half of the core.

60
00:08:00,819 --> 00:08:07,819
When enough neutrons are available to initiate the fission of fuel atoms, the assembly becomes

61
00:08:07,819 --> 00:08:20,159
self-sustaining and a wide range of nuclear data is recorded.

62
00:08:20,159 --> 00:08:26,659
The first split table machine was built in 1955, prompted by the complete lack of experimental

63
00:08:26,660 --> 00:08:32,560
information on fast reactor systems.

64
00:08:32,560 --> 00:08:40,279
These low-power machines achieve and maintain a self-sustaining chain reaction without producing

65
00:08:40,279 --> 00:08:51,840
a significant amount of power, less than 100 watts.

66
00:08:51,840 --> 00:08:57,879
These test reactors discover in a matter of months what non-test reactors take years of

67
00:08:57,879 --> 00:09:00,879
operation to discover.

68
00:09:00,879 --> 00:09:08,259
They can investigate specific reactors on a realistic scale, predict reactor performance,

69
00:09:08,259 --> 00:09:16,200
and operate at higher power densities than slow neutron reactors.

70
00:09:16,200 --> 00:09:22,379
Since they can tolerate a greater buildup of fission products, they hold the most promise

71
00:09:22,379 --> 00:09:29,020
for economic power production.

72
00:09:29,020 --> 00:09:36,180
To be competitive, the next family of fast-power reactors fueled with high-cost plutonium must

73
00:09:36,180 --> 00:09:40,580
generate at least 1,000 megawatts.

74
00:09:40,580 --> 00:09:46,580
It is therefore necessary to define many of the physical, chemical, nuclear and irradiation

75
00:09:46,580 --> 00:09:50,259
properties of the plutonium.

76
00:09:50,259 --> 00:09:56,520
Its use can only be meaningful in fast breeder reactors.

77
00:09:56,520 --> 00:10:02,320
Plutonium will dramatically affect power economics in the fuel cycle and will contribute to efforts

78
00:10:02,320 --> 00:10:26,040
of doubling the inventory of fissionable material in 10 to 20 years.

79
00:10:26,039 --> 00:10:32,639
This is the Zero Power Plutonium Reactor, a three and three quarter million dollar Mayan

80
00:10:32,639 --> 00:10:41,679
temple dedicated to exploring the frontiers of fast reactor physics.

81
00:10:41,679 --> 00:10:46,759
Experiments with this reactor will permit the construction of larger, more sophisticated

82
00:10:46,759 --> 00:10:53,659
power stations and will contribute information on how to multiply the world's supply of

83
00:10:53,659 --> 00:11:03,480
reactor fuel. ZPPR is a tool capable of studying large plutonium-fueled fast

84
00:11:03,480 --> 00:11:10,819
reactors, producing 1,000 megawatts of electrical power. It is the only facility

85
00:11:10,819 --> 00:11:17,539
with this capability. Its design is a sharp departure from previous

86
00:11:17,539 --> 00:11:20,559
containment designs.

87
00:11:23,659 --> 00:11:33,779
The facility is divided into two general areas, an underground mound area and a large support wing.

88
00:11:36,719 --> 00:11:44,500
The mound area is made up of a deep layer of compacted earth, which covers a 50-foot circular reactor cell,

89
00:11:44,500 --> 00:11:51,500
A fuel storage vault where the plutonium is stored.

90
00:11:51,500 --> 00:11:59,179
An equipment room and access and escape tunnels leading from the reactor cell.

91
00:11:59,179 --> 00:12:05,899
The support wing contains the reactor control room and office and other necessary facilities

92
00:12:05,899 --> 00:12:17,500
for the operation and maintenance of the reactor.

93
00:12:17,500 --> 00:12:24,319
The construction of ZPP-R, the fourth such argon split-table reactor, began in August

94
00:12:24,319 --> 00:12:27,419
of 1966.

95
00:12:27,419 --> 00:12:32,919
Heavily reinforced concrete was used to build the walls and floor of the reactor, forming

96
00:12:32,919 --> 00:12:42,519
a circular room 50 feet in diameter to form a solid foundation for the reactor

97
00:12:42,519 --> 00:12:48,939
cell and to ensure it against possible damage from earthquakes. Steel pilings

98
00:12:48,939 --> 00:12:59,379
were driven deep into the underlying lava bed. A uniquely designed cell roof

99
00:12:59,379 --> 00:13:03,700
was fabricated to provide backup safety containment for the reactor.

100
00:13:05,080 --> 00:13:11,100
An intricate web of steel cables knitted together to form a free-swinging hammock

101
00:13:11,100 --> 00:13:15,500
was lined with several layers of overlapping wire mesh.

102
00:13:18,980 --> 00:13:24,379
This hammock, designed to support a 16-foot-thick bed of sand and gravel,

103
00:13:24,379 --> 00:13:27,500
will retain any possible energy release

104
00:13:27,500 --> 00:13:30,480
from the combustion of large masses of plutonium.

105
00:13:35,740 --> 00:13:41,500
The containment roof was supplemented by a circular array of highly efficient filters

106
00:13:41,500 --> 00:13:45,480
to prevent any significant release of fission products.

107
00:13:45,480 --> 00:13:57,399
All installations were completed by the end of 1968

108
00:13:57,399 --> 00:14:00,659
and the reactor was ready to be loaded

109
00:14:00,659 --> 00:14:03,420
for the march to criticality.

110
00:14:15,480 --> 00:14:25,879
During reactor startup and operation, the reactor cell is separated from the outside environment by a series of blast and seal doors.

111
00:14:27,060 --> 00:14:32,560
This separation precaution ensures complete safety when the reactor is operating.

112
00:14:38,320 --> 00:14:43,639
There are many fundamental differences between fast reactors and their thermal counterparts.

113
00:14:45,480 --> 00:14:49,840
In a fast reactor, there is no reason to slow down the neutrons.

114
00:14:50,680 --> 00:14:54,560
We can eliminate the moderator and reduce the core size.

115
00:14:55,779 --> 00:15:00,680
In thermal cores, the reactor is contained in a pressure vessel.

116
00:15:01,680 --> 00:15:05,800
In a fast reactor, with the use of liquid sodium as coolant,

117
00:15:06,340 --> 00:15:09,100
the containment problems are dramatically reduced.

118
00:15:10,680 --> 00:15:14,580
The fast core is unmoderated and compact.

119
00:15:15,480 --> 00:15:21,779
consisting only of an assembly of fissionable fuel, structural material, and coolant.

120
00:15:22,899 --> 00:15:30,420
By operating at higher temperatures, we achieve a better efficiency in converting heat to electricity.

121
00:15:38,259 --> 00:15:45,399
The recipes, or fuel strategies, for mocking up fast core are drawn up and loaded in this workroom.

122
00:15:45,480 --> 00:15:55,480
Before being loaded into the reactor, the fuel is inspected in these negative pressure glove boxes.

123
00:15:57,480 --> 00:16:09,480
Fifteen hundred kilograms of the plutonium fuel, valued at 64 and one half million dollars, are stored in these bins.

124
00:16:09,480 --> 00:16:19,120
When pushed to 3,000 kilograms, this inventory will represent the largest mass of plutonium

125
00:16:19,120 --> 00:16:27,659
employed in any single critical facility. Because it is a prolific spontaneous emitter of neutrons,

126
00:16:27,659 --> 00:16:35,460
the plutonium is stored in small lots in these concrete blocks, which act as radiation shielding.

127
00:16:35,460 --> 00:16:44,460
It is clad in stainless steel plates and stored in airtight canisters.

128
00:16:44,460 --> 00:16:50,200
The initial experiments will be performed with volumes of 2,000 liters, demonstrating

129
00:16:50,200 --> 00:16:55,220
reactors in the three to 400 megawatt range.

130
00:16:55,220 --> 00:17:01,960
Future cores will exceed 6,000 liters, simulating reactors generating electrical power for a

131
00:17:01,960 --> 00:17:11,460
city of a million people. ZPPR can accommodate essentially any power reactor core, be it

132
00:17:11,460 --> 00:17:20,299
a metal, oxide, or carbide fueled core. By using metal fuel, the combinations assembled

133
00:17:20,299 --> 00:17:28,360
in this test reactor core are extensive. To build the core design, stainless steel tubes

134
00:17:28,360 --> 00:17:35,660
or fuel drawers are manually loaded in these glove boxes.

135
00:17:35,660 --> 00:17:41,140
These mock-up fuels are in the form of small plates, and their interchangeability makes

136
00:17:41,140 --> 00:17:47,860
this machine a very flexible experimental tool.

137
00:17:47,860 --> 00:17:54,440
The individual fuel drawers, perforated to reduce density, contain a specific combination

138
00:17:54,440 --> 00:17:57,900
of fissionable, structural, and coolant materials,

139
00:17:57,900 --> 00:18:02,920
such as blanket material, metallic sodium, carbon, and depleted uranium.

140
00:18:09,940 --> 00:18:14,880
After complete checks, they are transported to the reactor cell

141
00:18:14,880 --> 00:18:16,680
through a 50-foot conveyor tube.

142
00:18:24,440 --> 00:18:34,660
The core halves are loaded by stacking the fuel into separate rectangular lattices that

143
00:18:34,660 --> 00:18:37,880
are formed by cast steel knees.

144
00:18:37,880 --> 00:18:47,680
The lattices are held in position by I-beams bolted to the knees for stabilization.

145
00:18:47,680 --> 00:19:01,060
After they are loaded into the reactor matrix assembly, the drawers are again checked.

146
00:19:01,060 --> 00:19:07,720
For safety and convenience, the loading is performed from a seven foot wide platform,

147
00:19:07,720 --> 00:19:12,640
which is retracted when the tables are drawn together.

148
00:19:12,640 --> 00:19:19,280
The tops of the tables are at the same elevation as the main floor of the cell.

149
00:19:19,280 --> 00:19:25,300
The entire assembly rests upon a thick slab of reinforced concrete anchored in a four-foot

150
00:19:25,300 --> 00:19:28,040
pin.

151
00:19:28,040 --> 00:19:34,580
The movable table travels on roller bearings and is driven through the 84-inch separation

152
00:19:34,580 --> 00:19:39,240
by three different speed closure motors.

153
00:19:39,240 --> 00:19:44,240
Each table is designed to support 120 tons.

154
00:19:44,240 --> 00:19:47,480
When they are closed, they form a single matrix

155
00:19:47,480 --> 00:19:51,700
10 feet square and 8 feet deep.

156
00:19:51,700 --> 00:19:57,680
When larger cores are to be simulated, the matrix will be extended to 14 feet square.

157
00:19:57,680 --> 00:20:13,620
Although operated at less than 100 watts of power, supplementary radiation shielding is

158
00:20:13,620 --> 00:20:20,440
provided by large retractable personnel shields which can be positioned in front of each half

159
00:20:20,440 --> 00:20:38,080
of the reactor.

160
00:20:38,080 --> 00:20:43,840
All control aspects of the reactor are performed in this room.

161
00:20:43,839 --> 00:20:50,839
This interlocking console monitors the reactor during startup and operation.

162
00:20:52,199 --> 00:20:58,459
The reactor is controlled by safety and fuel-bearing control rods, which can be driven into each

163
00:20:58,459 --> 00:21:02,339
matrix.

164
00:21:02,339 --> 00:21:09,339
Duplicate instrumentation has been installed to provide malfunctioning backup.

165
00:21:13,839 --> 00:21:24,159
ZPPR will measure the effects of sodium loss, core temperature on the system's reactivity,

166
00:21:24,159 --> 00:21:31,279
and produce accurate definition of the neutron spectrum for the core under study.

167
00:21:31,279 --> 00:21:37,159
This broad range of nuclear analysis will predict the behavior and economy of future

168
00:21:37,159 --> 00:21:40,639
breeder reactors.

169
00:21:40,640 --> 00:21:45,640
To respond to this data, these instruments will channel the information to a digital

170
00:21:45,640 --> 00:21:51,759
computer which will automatically collect and process the data.

171
00:21:51,759 --> 00:22:02,400
The computer will also be used to control experiments whenever practical.

172
00:22:02,400 --> 00:22:08,920
These new techniques will eliminate the past need for frequent reactor shutdown to study

173
00:22:08,920 --> 00:22:16,580
core performance.

174
00:22:16,580 --> 00:22:25,259
The search for the critical mass in ZPP-R began on March 27, 1969.

175
00:22:25,259 --> 00:22:36,920
The plutonium fuel was constantly inspected to ensure against contamination during storage.

176
00:22:36,920 --> 00:22:41,980
Repeated checks and verifications were made to ensure that the fuel drawers were correctly

177
00:22:41,980 --> 00:22:46,560
collated and loaded into the reactor in proper sequence.

178
00:22:46,559 --> 00:23:07,440
The core halves were repeatedly drawn together and the data plotted on the critical approach

179
00:23:07,440 --> 00:23:13,879
curve.

180
00:23:13,880 --> 00:23:20,980
The daily loading followed an habitual schedule, checking the operation of the counters, loading

181
00:23:20,980 --> 00:23:29,740
the fuel drawers, placing the fuel into the reactor, moving the tables together, plotting

182
00:23:29,740 --> 00:23:33,600
the critical approach curve.

183
00:23:33,600 --> 00:23:43,660
After 16 days of loading, the core contained 340 kilograms of plutonium.

184
00:23:43,660 --> 00:23:49,040
approach curve suggested less than 20 more kilograms for the reactor to go

185
00:23:49,040 --> 00:23:57,420
critical. On the morning of April 18th, the 17th day of loading, the instrument

186
00:23:57,420 --> 00:24:06,820
normalization is made. The decision? Add 12.1 kilograms to the core. Total

187
00:24:06,820 --> 00:24:13,140
inventory, 352.81 kilograms.

188
00:24:13,660 --> 00:24:17,259
The tables are again brought together.

189
00:24:39,259 --> 00:24:43,160
The loading does not achieve criticality.

190
00:24:43,660 --> 00:25:02,740
In the afternoon, another loading, this time 4.53 kilograms of plutonium.

191
00:25:02,740 --> 00:25:12,160
Total fuel load, now 357.34 kilograms.

192
00:25:12,160 --> 00:25:20,259
final checks and once again the control room vigil begins

193
00:26:12,160 --> 00:26:38,680
At 325 on April 18th, 1969, ZPPR became critical with a plutonium mass of 357.34 kilograms.

194
00:26:38,680 --> 00:26:45,560
Its baptism into the field of fast reactor physics was now history.

195
00:26:45,560 --> 00:26:50,960
This march to criticality for ZPPR was merely the first step in its day-to-day research

196
00:26:50,960 --> 00:26:56,799
existence in the liquid metal fast breeder reactor program.

197
00:26:56,799 --> 00:27:02,120
Its future performance will contribute vital information for the eventual construction

198
00:27:02,120 --> 00:27:07,960
of plutonium-fueled fast breeder power reactors.

199
00:27:07,960 --> 00:27:14,960
When perfected, these perpetual energy machines will serve us not in terms of decades, but

200
00:27:15,140 --> 00:27:22,140
in terms of centuries. This criticality in the Idaho desert has been one more test along

201
00:27:23,420 --> 00:27:30,420
the experimental road of fast reactor physics. There are many more to come.
